+    # 8.4d's live gate, 2026-09-08 08:22Z on m910q. The sentence NAMES a command,
+    # so the order of its arguments is a claim like any other, and this fork's
+    # console answers to exactly one of the two. Measured by sending both at the
+    # worldserver console of the running Tortoise install:
+    #     `rndbot Ahi level 60` -> "Ahi: level - character not found"
+    #     `rndbot level Ahi 60` -> "level: Ahi - ok"
+    # The reply's shape is `<cmd>: <charname> - <result>`, so the first spelling
+    # read the BOT's name as the command word and then looked for a character
+    # called `level`. A sentence carrying that order would tell a reader to send
+    # a line this server cannot parse -- and its refusal would look like the
+    # level command refusing, which is not what happens: the accepted spelling
+    # answers `ok` and leaves the level alone (Ahi held 19 for 60 s afterwards
+    # while its position kept being written).
+    assert "`.rndbot level <bot>`" in said, said
+    assert "`.rndbot <bot> level`" not in said, said
 
 
